A few years ago I was working as a computer tech at 3M.  I had the chance to install/upgrade/build and otherwise muck with tons of differnt kinds of machines.  I worked on Macs, IBM's, PS/2 (Not the playstation), UNIX, Silicon Graphics etc.<P>When I would upgrade a computer they would usually get the cheapest type of memory we could find.  (for about $100 per megabyte 6 years ago hehe)  About 10% of the PNY memoory we were shipped was sent back.<P>However things change.  Over the years PNY has become a 'generic' memory company.  Their product is as good as any other 'generic' memory out on the market today.  However I am a hardcore gamer/computer geek.  If you want really good memory you buy <A HREF="http://www.mushkin.com/cgi-bin/mushkin.storefront" TARGET=_blank>Mushkin products</A><P>If you consider the fact that the average processor today runs at about 1GHz and memory is stuck back at 133MHz (If you are lucky) then you run into a situation where the processor can do 8 clock cycles while the memory can fetch only 1 32 bit chunk of data.  This leaves the processor just sitting there spinning it's wheels very fast waiting for more information.<P>A small bump in the preformance of memory can make a very nice bump in system preformance.  CAS2:2:2 at 150MHz, 7ns makes a big difference, if your motherboard will support it.<P>So when i was bad-mouthing PNY I was just punching 'generic' memory in general.  Use it, love it, stick it in your mom's computer and don't worry about it.  But if you want to turbo charge your computer buy the high end brand.  (just like with cars & bikes)<P>/geek mode off<BR>Joe
